<h2>Which platforms actually matter for small business</h2>

<p>You do not need to be everywhere. In fact, trying to maintain a presence on five platforms is a fast track to burnout. Pick one or two and do them well.</p>

<p>For most local service businesses, these are the platforms worth your time:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Instagram.</strong> Visual, local-friendly, and heavily used for discovery. If your work is visual at all (fitness, beauty, trades, food, events), Instagram is a strong pick.</li>
  <li><strong>Facebook.</strong> Still dominant for local communities and the 35-plus demographic. Facebook Groups and your Business Page drive local visibility. And Facebook Marketplace can be a <a href="/blog/lead-generation-service-business">lead source</a> in itself.</li>
  <li><strong>Google Business Profile.</strong> Technically not social media, but posts here appear in search results. Free visibility to people actively searching for what you offer. Do not sleep on this one.</li>
  <li><strong>LinkedIn.</strong> Best for B2B, consulting, professional services. If your clients are other businesses, LinkedIn posts build authority quickly.</li>
  <li><strong>TikTok.</strong> High reach potential but demands video content. Worth exploring if you are comfortable on camera and targeting a younger audience.</li>
</ul>

<p>The right answer depends on where your customers spend their time. If you are a plumber in the suburbs, TikTok is probably not your first priority. If you run a photography studio, Instagram is non-negotiable.</p>

<h2>Content pillars: never run out of ideas</h2>

<p>A content pillar is a recurring theme you post about regularly. Having three to five pillars means you never stare at a blank screen wondering what to say.</p>

<p>Here is an example set for a personal trainer:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Educational.</strong> Quick tips on form, nutrition, or recovery.</li>
  <li><strong>Social proof.</strong> Client transformations, testimonials, before and after results.</li>
  <li><strong>Behind the scenes.</strong> Your morning routine, gym setup, how you plan sessions.</li>
  <li><strong>Engagement.</strong> Polls, questions, "this or that" comparisons.</li>
  <li><strong>Promotional.</strong> Offers, new programs, booking availability.</li>
</ul>

<p>Each week, you pull from each pillar at least once. This gives your feed variety while keeping it focused. Your audience knows what to expect from you, and you never have to invent a topic from nothing.</p>

<blockquote><p>Content pillars do not limit your creativity. They give it a framework to operate within.</p></blockquote>

