<h2>The minimum viable posting schedule</h2>

<p>Forget what the marketing gurus tell you about posting three times a day. For a small business, consistency beats frequency every time. Here is a realistic schedule that works:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Instagram:</strong> 3 to 4 posts per week, plus a few Stories if you have time.</li>
  <li><strong>Facebook:</strong> 3 posts per week. Share to relevant local groups where appropriate.</li>
  <li><strong>Google Business Profile:</strong> 1 post per week. Keep it simple. A photo, a sentence, and a call to action.</li>
  <li><strong>LinkedIn:</strong> 2 posts per week. Longer-form, thoughtful content performs best here.</li>
</ul>

<p>That is roughly 10 pieces of content per week if you are on two platforms. At 5 to 10 minutes per post during your batching session, you are looking at under 2 hours a week. Compare that to the 5 to 7 hours most business owners spend when they post manually and reactively.</p>

<h2>Scheduling tools vs. posting live</h2>

<p>Some people worry that scheduled posts feel less authentic. Here is the truth: your audience cannot tell the difference. A post that goes out at 8am on Tuesday is a post that goes out at 8am on Tuesday, whether you pressed "publish" in the moment or scheduled it three days earlier.</p>

<p>What scheduling does give you is the ability to post at optimal times (even when you are asleep or with a client), maintain consistency without daily effort, and preview your feed before anything goes live.</p>

<p>The one thing scheduling does not replace is engagement. You still need to reply to comments, respond to DMs, and interact with your community. But that takes 10 minutes a day, not an hour. Block it into your routine and keep it separate from content creation.</p>

<p>When your scheduling tool is connected to your broader marketing system, things get even more efficient. Posts can be tied to campaigns, <a href="/blog/landing-pages-that-convert">landing pages</a>, and <a href="/blog/automate-follow-up">follow-up automations</a>. The content works harder because it is part of a bigger machine.</p>

